# Cloudflare Workers static-assets reads this the same way Pages does.
# NOTE: when several rules match one request, same-named headers are
# CONCATENATED, not overridden -- observed live: a /* no-cache rule plus
# the /assets/* rule shipped assets with "no-cache, public,
# max-age=31536000, immutable" stacked in one header. Browsers resolved
# it correctly (immutable won in practice) but the header contradicted
# itself on its face. So the rules below are mutually exclusive by
# construction: no catch-all, HTML paths named explicitly.

# The SPA's HTML entry points -- the only document URLs a real visitor
# ever loads (the app has no URL routing; tabs are client state, and
# magic links land on "/?token=...", which is still path /). The HTML's
# content changes every deploy (it points at the newly-hashed asset
# URLs), so it must never be cached long: `no-cache` still allows
# storing but forces an etag revalidation (cheap 304) on every use in
# browsers. Browsers are this file's whole jurisdiction: the Workers
# assets edge cache ignores these headers and is superseded atomically
# by each deploy (proven live 2026-08-15). A hand-typed deep link
# served by not_found_handling=single-page-application falls outside
# these rules and gets the platform default -- acceptable, since
# nothing ever links such a path. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md's "Frontend
# cache behavior" for the full caching model.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# /assets/* filenames are content-hashed by Vite (index-<hash>.js) --
# the URL itself changes whenever the content does, so "cache forever"
# is correct, not stale: a new deploy is a new URL, never the same URL
# with different bytes. Without this, the default `must-revalidate`
# response makes every repeat visit pay a full round-trip to confirm
# what's already sitting in the browser's cache unchanged.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The OG share image and email mark are stable, un-hashed filenames --
# still safe to cache long, just not "immutable" (a future redeploy
# could legitimately replace either at the same URL).
/brand/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
